Author: frank <hellmann@pik-potsdam.de>
Date:   Mon Nov 23 01:17:10 2026 +0100

    runtime: a membership replays the methods whose variable's lower bound the member meets
    
    A method whose signature has a type variable with a lower bound (`h(::Ph{T}) where T >: S`) is selected, once `S`
    joins `X`, at a call type that names the set (`Ph{X}`): a lower bound is the one contravariant place of a
    signature, where a type under the member, not the set, carries the membership's effect. Such a method names no set,
    so the set-to-methods index did not list it, no walk replayed it, and the dispatch caches, `hasmethod`, `applicable`
    and the callers' edges kept the old selection, declared or taken back; and a lookup's worlds were narrowed only by
    the entries of the query's types, so a match through `S`'s entry was valid before it (E-542's pattern 5). Now the
    index lists each method with a lower bound at a covariant place under the key `nothing` (twenty of Base's methods,
    `T >: Missing`, `T >: Nothing`); the declaration's and the removal's walks replay, at the method's own signature,
    each such method whose bound meets the member in the walk's world (`lower_bound_targets`), as they replay a method
    at a bound the signature sets; the replay signatures for the library's check name them; and a match's or an
    absence's worlds are narrowed by the entries of the signature's lower bounds too (`jl_sig_lower_bounds`).
    
    Reference: compiler §5.5 (a membership invalidates as a method does), §5.3.
    Test: experiments/implementation/w171/gates/n43_lower_bound_replay.jl.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

